{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbTAPAXtYPaUB97AmyaY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The candidate passes the self-check, which printed `OK`. Two warnings came with it: R-hat can't be computed from a one-chain fit, and the leave-one-out score was flagged unreliable (8.9% of trials had high Pareto k, against a 1% tolerance). That second warning is probably because the smoke fit has only 100 draws, but I haven't confirmed it. The full admission fit will settle it, along with convergence and novelty, which the self-check doesn't test.\n\n**Model chosen:** `personal_decisiveness_mirror_streak_lapse`. It is rank 1 and statistically tied with the incumbent (5.2 ± 12.3 nats behind).\n\n**The one change:** In the original, switching too often and switching too rarely count against a sequence equally. Now switching more often than a person's ideal rate costs a shared fraction (`omega`) of what switching less often costs. If `omega` is below 1, people forgive over-alternation more than streakiness. The penalty stays continuous and smooth at the ideal rate, because the squared gap is zero there. Everything else is unchanged.\n\n**Why this change:** It targets the critique finding that survived the multiple-comparison correction. Among pairs where both sequences alternate often (rate ≥ 0.6), people chose the more alternating sequence 59.4% of the time; the incumbent predicts 47.7% (q = 0.016). A penalty that treats both directions equally can't produce that gap.\n\n**Smoke-fit result:** ELPD-LOO of −2298.1, against −2301.4 for the incumbent's full fit. That suggests it is competitive, but a 100-draw, one-chain fit is not a real comparison.\n\nThe three files are in `.../experiment2/model_loop/iter_3/candidate_5/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`: `asymmetric_ideal_rate_decisiveness`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input